{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-48646",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "At $x=2,$ the left-sided limit is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}g(x) & =g(2) \\\\  & ={3}^{2a(2)+1} \\\\  & ={3}^{4a+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAt $x=2,$ the right-sided limit is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(2x+5) \\\\  & =2(2)+5 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{4a+1} & =9 \\\\ {3}^{4a+1} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ 4a+1 & =2 \\\\ 4a & =1 \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$ for $a=\\frac{1}{4}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
